CI/CD Webhook Setup — steps
1. Add Jenkinsfile to GitHub repo
    -   Keep it at the root of the project.
    -   It contains: Maven Build → Docker Build → Trivy Scan → Docker Push.
2. Configure Jenkins
    -   Job → Configure → Pipeline
    -   Select Pipeline script from SCM
    -   SCM: Git
        Repository: your GitHub repo
        Branch: */master
    -   Script Path: Jenkinsfile
3. Enable Webhook Trigger
    -   Jenkins → Build Triggers
    -   Select GitHub hook trigger for GITScm polling
    -   Don't enable Poll SCM.

4. Expose local Jenkins

    -   Since Jenkins is running on localhost:9090, GitHub cannot directly access it.
    -   Run:    ngrok http 9090
    -   Example:    https://abc123.ngrok-free.app

5. Configure GitHub Webhook

    GitHub → Repository → Settings → Webhooks → Add webhook
    Payload URL:    https://abc123.ngrok-free.app/github-webhook/
    Content type: application/json
    Select Just the push event
    Enable Active

6. Test

                    Developer
                        ↓ git push
                    GitHub
                        ↓ Webhook
                      ngrok
                        ↓
                    Jenkins
                        ↓
                    Maven → Docker Build → Trivy → Docker Push
* Important

Jenkinsfile stays in GitHub.
Developers don't manually send the Jenkinsfile with every push.
Jenkins reads the Jenkinsfile from the commit that triggered the build.


What ngrok does

Your Jenkins is running on:

http://localhost:9090

But GitHub is on the internet and cannot reach your localhost.

ngrok creates a temporary public URL that forwards requests to your local Jenkins:

GitHub
   ↓
https://xxxx.ngrok-free.app
   ↓
ngrok
   ↓
http://localhost:9090
   ↓
Jenkins